 something like this where the  surface isn't quite as flat as we'd like  what will happen of course is that the  tip will crash into that if you're not  continually adjusting the site that's  why this constant current mode is so  much better because as we move the tip  across it will detect oh hang on there's  a  bigger increase in current i must be  getting closer to the surface got to  pull back pull back maintain the current  constant and then continue on its way  okay so  nanoscience is not i would argue and i  think anybody who's a nanoscientist  would argue this as well is not this  wonderful new type of physics or new  type of science or  groundbreaking new paradigm it's not  like quantum mechanics it's not like  relativity but what it is is a natural  convergence of  physics chemistry biology engineering  all right down at the single atom at the  single molecule level  and so although nano of the over the  past decade or so has really exploded  into this wonderfully new as you say  buzzword it's  it's been around for a lot longer than  that really the  nano stems from the the word nanometer  and a nanometer is uh a billionth of a  meter  the question is well what does that mean  in sort of terms we can get to get our  heads around which is so more connected  to the real world so i guess many people  will have seen microscope images of red  blood cells for example and those are  off the order of 5 000 nanometers across  these  really neat movies videos you see of  bacteria multiplying so bacteria are  typically hundreds to thousands of  nanometers across so we're working on  length scales much much smaller than  than that sort of one of the molecules  that's custom built for nanoscience and  has has really been used by very many  groups in nanoscience to do a range of  remarkable things is c60 so c60 is  literally a molecular football  so you have um  [Music]  a structure that looks very much like  this this is a conventionally stitched  football so you can 